#{[#edb/ref 17592186050531 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084147} :activity/id #uuid "4631a63f-ad43-556f-95cf-d62364c255c3" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084390 :tutorial/content "A curve or function is defined parametrically if $x$ and $y$ are given in terms of a third parameter $t.$ This can often give rise to some interesting shapes. \n\nFor example, we can define a curve as \n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nIf we plot the curve, it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b3/b3d085ad763c368d7fafaca878c364fc13a0c83ca73b2fd17a2f349cb402a16e.png)\n\nIf we're given a parametric curve, how do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}?$ The answer is to use the chain rule. \n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}t}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange this formula into a nicer version, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{1}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)}{{x}^{'}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use the above formula to calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for the given parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3t^2-3, \\qquad y'(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)}{{x}^{'}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the curve is defined in terms of the parameter $t,$ the derivative is also defined in terms of $t.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084148}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083054 :question/id #uuid "15c029df-b002-51cc-87db-a94d40a02771" :question/math-academy-id "e-595" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad  0\\leq t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\n![](images/2e/2ec95dd061fa6b7f09c9f899a1f6483b4ea7d5127372ddd6eefb74d6ed10c14c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :question/id #uuid "148e5378-9177-59ba-9b1e-3633c940babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-596" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{-t}}{(1+t){e}^{t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+t){e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2(1)}(1+1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Parametric Curves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083472}] [#edb/ref 17592186050508 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173088} :activity/id #uuid "be5d259b-c5cc-5faf-98aa-a40df75803b1" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173252 :tutorial/content "Consider the surface integral of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\n \nover the parametric surface $S,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 2v, \\: -u \\big\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is the unit square in the $uv$-plane given by\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\: | \\: 0\\leq u\\leq 1,\\: 0\\leq v\\leq 1\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the surface integral of $f$ over $S.$\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the surface integral of $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=-u} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(2v)^{2}+(-u)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+8\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172130 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171710 :question/id #uuid "38dc8ce4-246d-54bd-a400-48c801d9387f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12344" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1ef5ddc89d7846d1126c60f34fc400669d23d6ca16c2b622addaf2916955ed27.svg)\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: -v, \\: v \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v) \\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where $u \\geq 0.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(z-y)\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =v-(-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4\\sqrt{2}uv\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171711 :question/id #uuid "fce43073-3f48-5e12-a5c0-ecef3a31a57a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12346" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} (x-y)\\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx = u+v, \\qquad \ny = u-v, \\qquad \nz = v\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =x-y\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2v\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,2v\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173256 :tutorial/content "Consider a continuous scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ and a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ defined for $(u,v) \\in D,$ where $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ \n\nIf our surface is \"sufficiently smooth,\" we can define the **surface integral** of $f$ over $S.$ Let's discuss how we might construct such an integral.\n\nFor the sake of simplicity, let's assume that our region $D$ is rectangular. We start by creating a partition $P$ of $D$ in the usual way:\n\n$$\nP = \\left\\{u_0, u_1, \\ldots, u_m\\right\\} \\times \\left\\{v_0, v_1, \\ldots, v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nOur partition is shown schematically below.\n\n![](images/6a/6a15c7b587503c82a57d0734c5bc5fda990824ad162724236868461cd92e4fbe.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v$ of the small rectangle highlighted in the diagram corresponds to a particular patch $S_{ij}$ of area $\\delta S_{ij}$ on our surface. \n\n![](images/1a/1a8774b7aa366d3328b6f81cfe7315b7784a1f5783f14c1f19f83a459cef5434.png)\n\n \n\nLet $P_{ij}^*$ be a point on the surface within the patch $S_{ij}.$ We consider the product \n\n$$\nf(P_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nIf we compute this product for all patches and add them together, we get the Riemann sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n f(P_{ij}^*)\\cdot\\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $\\delta u, \\delta v\\to 0$ gives our definition of the surface integral of $f$ over $S,$ which we denote as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFrom previous discussions, we know that the area of the small patch can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\delta S_{ij} \\approx \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta u\\delta v = \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta A\n$$\n\nIt follows that we can calculate our surface integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThis formula is also valid when $D$ is non-rectangular. \n\nLet's see an example of applying this in practice." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173080}}] :activity/title "Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172188}] [#edb/ref 17592186050523 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176859} :activity/id #uuid "5f43bfbe-a51c-5be9-8b78-730c73414b85" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177066 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nThis graph also has infinitely many zeros, and there is an infinite number of asymptotes of the function. We can write down general expressions for these properties, assuming $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the zeros of the function are $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\dots.$ \n\nWe write down a general expression for the zeros as follows:\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nThe function has an asymptote when $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ and the function is periodic with period $\\pi.$ Therefore, a general equation for the asymptotes of the graph is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175695 :question/id #uuid "2f4571af-99cf-5a4a-9998-fb6a0f7d6be9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11004" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The zeros of the function are at $x = \\pi n.$\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x = 2\\pi n.$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm2\\pi,\\ldots,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has an asymptote at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2$ and the period is $\\pi.$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2 + \\pi n.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177067 :tutorial/content "Recall again the graph of the function $y=\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nWe've seen that the function $y=\\tan x$ is periodic, where the period is $\\pi.$\n\n![](images/e9/e911ee6c5bf6c178812c3079633544653066eb6371c4e6c38e3d2df51e622742.png)\n\n   The periodicity of $y=\\tan x$ means that $\\tan\\left( x+n\\pi\\right) = \\tan\\left( x\\right)$ for any number $x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176050 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175696 :question/id #uuid "f4649856-d777-5468-a7f4-df50479e36d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11005" :question/problem "If $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2$ for some value of $x,$ then find the value of $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\tan x$ is $\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\tan (x+n\\pi).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x & =tan⁡(x+(-2)\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2,$ we get that $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right) = -2.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :question/id #uuid "0b146642-914c-52ce-9939-86752e95dcd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11013" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac{2}{3},$ find the value of $\\tan (-x+7\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan{x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x+7\\pi)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177083 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, tangent is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the tangent function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa332c1b04ea9ce36e09fae4368d7964d21d3a36cadb115ea2e1e89518af543e.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of tangent in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/title "The Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176876}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176109}] [#edb/ref 17592186050482 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199250} :activity/id #uuid "8416c2c4-cff0-5744-94c6-6f83c5fc5709" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199451 :tutorial/content "Suppose that\n\n$$\\mathbf T: (u,v) \\to \\left(x(u,v),y(u,v)\\right)$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = D,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\nWe've already seen how the area of a non-trivial region $D$ can be computed using the change of variables formula: $$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$\n\nWe can extend this formula to compute the integral of a function $f(x,y)$ over $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for the integral of a single-variable function $f(x)$ over $D = (a,b){:}$\n\n$$\\int\\limits_D f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} f(x(u))\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula is similar to when we were restricting our attention to areas in the plane. The only difference now is that we must also express the integrand $f(x,y)$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198235 :question/id #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7285" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq 4x+2y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u = 4x+2y$ and $v=y - x,$ write the following integral in terms of $u$ and $v.$ \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iint\\limits_D \\sin \\left(3(x+y)\\right)  \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/b8/b83369a26291155dbb8a5031d91fcf869cff259b7c170b6a15d58d317cee934d.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/46/461f793c9e8d6c3e5e5f67f846bade4acea9d40dc6c3975659d60167c33f6579.png)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}))|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}sin\\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198015 :question/id #uuid "e8d75369-b381-53d3-b3af-37c6109c15a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12327" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{y-x}{x+y} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4 \\right\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n![](images/03/039187200f0587407c67e8663de037df0a9e71155e91c4ea497e9d10d66d9119.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n![](images/e7/e7adae8ec845db2e091e9813a338b05895ebca800371df4841d327739863b863.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198016 :question/id #uuid "1ad4ddff-b776-50d9-b698-c0172ee9d3ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-12328" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D (2x+y)y^2 \\:\\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $\\left(\\dfrac32,0\\right) ,$ $\\left(\\dfrac52,0\\right) ,$ $(1,3),$ and $(0,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/03/039187200f0587407c67e8663de037df0a9e71155e91c4ea497e9d10d66d9119.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\n\\qquad\nv=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/88/88c92ebc206481c3ef86661ee413358bb060cb85f642148c609de0bf1a9da2d1.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(5,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2\\cdot 1+3,3) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(0,3) & =(2\\cdot 0+3,3) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y){y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Integrand"}}] :activity/title "Computing Double Integrals Using a Change of Variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198479}]}
